from assessment_writer import write_review
def compact(sid,title,rows,appraisal):
    write_review(sid,title,[(a,b.split(' | '),c) for a,b,c in rows],appraisal)

compact('MF-MASTERS-2019','MASTERS: blunted muscle-mass gains, without a significant strength difference',[
('Introduction; NCT02308228','Test whether metformin augments response to resistance training. | Registry primary outcome is type II fiber area change; whole-body and thigh measures are separate outcomes. | Hypertrophy and strength in older adults.','Do not label every mass endpoint primary.'),
('Methods; Table 1','Community-dwelling nondiabetic adults, eligibility age at least 65; median age about 69. | Kentucky and Alabama; independently mobile, predominantly White, generally high functioning. | 109 randomized: 54 metformin, 55 placebo; 94 completed: 46 and 48.','Not a trial restricted to frail adults with sarcopenia.'),
('Interventions','Metformin tablets, 850 mg. | One daily for 7 days then 850 mg twice daily, with 2-week wash-in and 14 weeks of training. | Supervised progressive resistance training three days weekly in both arms. | Sixteen weeks of medication, strength baseline after initial training adaptation.','The comparison is exercise plus drug versus exercise plus placebo.'),
('Methods','Placebo with identical training. | Parallel randomized allocation. | GAP: Permutation details are in Appendix S5, not successfully acquired. | Double-blind study. | Concurrent treatment during the same training program.','No metformin-only or no-training group was included.'),
('Methods; registry; Figures 2–4','Type II fiber area is registered primary; DXA, CT and strength are additional outcomes. | Biopsy histology, DXA mass, CT area and dynamometry. | Baseline and end of trial; strength change from week 4 to 16. | Muscle structure and performance, not disability, falls or survival. | NR: No established clinically important threshold for the reported percent mass changes.','Different measures of hypertrophy need not agree.'),
('Figures 2–3; corrected Table 2','DXA total lean mass +0.41% metformin versus +1.95% placebo; thigh mass +0.45% versus +3.90%. | DXA 46 versus 48; fiber area 30 per arm; corrected relative-strength analysis 44 per arm. | Between-arm P=.003 for total lean mass, P<.001 for thigh mass; type II fiber area +14.5% versus +18.5%, P=.610. | Knee extension strength +15.3% versus +23.1%, P=.055; corrected relative strength +14.8% versus +18.9%, P=.30. | SDs rather than confidence intervals for many changes; lean-mass SD 2.25% and 2.69%.','Mass attenuation is supported; a statistically significant strength loss is not.'),
('Results; Methods statistics','15/109 discontinued, including six due to adverse events. | Observed-data completer analysis; endpoint samples smaller for CT and biopsies. | GAP: Detailed pill/exercise adherence in inaccessible Appendix S1. | Thirteen participants outside BMI target were randomized; five completed fewer than 37 sessions; selected high-quality biopsies analyzed. | Analytic sample 94, not full ITT. | Person; bilateral measurements and multiple fibers do not create independent participants.','Completer selection and endpoint sampling limit causal interpretation.'),
('Results; Appendix S1 reference','Adverse events collected; main report compares event occurrence. | 109 randomized, with 94 completing; exact exposed safety denominator not separately established. | More events with metformin, chi-square 9.82, P=.002. | GAP: Six adverse-event discontinuations overall; full arm-specific event and withdrawal table unavailable. | Sixteen weeks.','Safety cannot be summarized as equal between groups.'),
('Methods; Results; corrigendum','Completer analysis, small mechanistic subsets and baseline sex/strength differences. | Randomization helps; percent-change analysis does not remove all selection concerns. | Registered primary fiber area result is null despite positive secondary mass findings. | Many outcomes and tests without a global multiplicity adjustment. | Modest sample; strength uncertainty includes no effect. | Corrigendum replaces the relative-strength row and updates Appendix S2.','Use corrected relative strength, not the stale original P=.188.'),
('Eligibility; Discussion','Generally healthy independently mobile older adults undertaking training. | 1700 mg/day with exercise; not a direct test of intermittent self-dosing or other formulations. | Training response, not treatment of established severe sarcopenia. | Cannot infer that metformin cancels all benefits of exercise or shortens life.','Scope the adverse interaction to the measured adaptations.'),
('Registry; current inventory','MASTERS, NCT02308228. | Corrigendum belongs to this trial; CP018 reviews it. | No additional independent RCT is added by the correction or review. | Current inventory reconciliation only.','Keep MET-PREVENT separate because its population and question differ.'),
('Byline; funding and conflicts','R. Grace Walton, Cory Dungan and colleagues. | Universities of Kentucky and Alabama at Birmingham. | NIA R01AG046920 and NCATS institutional awards. | NR: Product donation not established by the inspected main report. | Authors report no conflicts.','Funding disclosure is separate from scientific validity.'),
('Main XML; correction XML; registry','Published Aging Cell 2019, DOI 10.1111/acel.13039, with 2020 corrigendum. | Full report, actual correction and current registry acquired. | GAP: Five article appendices not recovered through the attempted PMC URLs; posted registry protocol is available. | Current NCT02308228 record confirms primary fiber-area endpoint. | Correction changes relative strength: placebo 3.89 to 4.62 kg/kg, metformin 4.60 to 5.23; P=.30. | November 3–4, 2026.','The historical correction is incorporated, not merely noted.'),
('G-METFORMIN physical-function chapter','Exercise adaptation and aging claims. | Supports reduced gains in some muscle-mass measures; does not support improved strength. | Authors emphasize blunted hypertrophy; editorial wording retains null primary fiber-area and nonsignificant strength findings.','No general anti-aging efficacy inference follows.'),
('Synthesis','Metformin did not augment resistance-training response in this trial. | Long-term functional consequences and responses in other metabolic states remain uncertain. | Larger prespecified functional trials with complete adherence and harms could clarify the balance.','Exercise remains a cointervention, not an outcome to abandon.')
], 'A useful randomized interaction study, limited by completer analysis and multiple outcomes. The registered primary fiber-area endpoint was null; DXA and CT support attenuation of some muscle-mass gains. The actual corrigendum is incorporated into relative-strength estimates.')

compact('MF-METPREVENT-2025','MET-PREVENT: no walking-speed benefit and poor tolerability in frail older adults',[
('Summary; Methods','Test efficacy and safety for physical performance in probable sarcopenia with prefrailty/frailty. | Primary endpoint specified as adjusted 4-meter walk speed at 4 months. | Clinical mobility, not lifespan.','The tested group differs markedly from healthy exercise volunteers.'),
('Methods; Table 1; flow Figure 1','Adults at least 65, mean 80.4 years; 58% women. | Two northeast England NHS trusts; walk speed below 0.8 m/s and low strength or slow chair rise; diabetes and eGFR below 45 excluded. | 268 expressed interest, 112 assessed, 72 randomized 36 per arm; 71 exposed, 70 with final data.','Probable sarcopenia did not require confirmed low muscle mass.'),
('Procedures; Discussion','Aurobindo immediate-release metformin 500-mg tablets. | Oral 500 mg three times daily from initiation, without dose titration, for four months. | No standardized training cointervention. | Outcomes four months; records followed for 28 days after last visit.','Poor tolerability may be regimen- and population-specific.'),
('Randomization and masking','Matching lactose/microcrystalline-cellulose placebo. | 1:1 via central web system. | Minimization by sex and baseline walk speed with 30% random element. | Participants, investigators and assessors masked; identical bottles. | Concurrent trial August 2021–September 2022 enrollment.','These methods reduce selection and measurement bias.'),
('Outcomes; statistical analysis','Primary adjusted walk speed; strength, function, body composition, quality of life and daily activities secondary. | Fastest of two timed 4-meter walks from standing start. | Four months. | Clinically relevant physical performance. | Trial powered for 0.1 m/s difference using its cited meaningful-change estimate.','A measured speed difference is more direct than a molecular aging marker.'),
('Table 2, PDF p7','Final means 0.57 m/s metformin versus 0.58 placebo. | 34 versus 36 complete cases. | Adjusted difference 0.001 m/s, 95% CI −0.06 to 0.06; P=.96. | Between-arm difference; per-protocol 19 versus 28 gave 0.02 m/s, CI −0.05 to 0.10. | Primary interval excludes the 0.1 m/s benefit targeted in the design.','The trial provides evidence against that size of benefit for this regimen.'),
('Results; Figure 1','Two metformin participants absent from final follow-up, none placebo. | Complete-case ITT; high retention despite medication discontinuation. | Median adherence 82% versus 92%; suboptimal adherence more frequent with metformin. | 13/34 metformin versus 5/36 placebo discontinued medication but continued follow-up. | All randomized for stated ITT; 70 complete cases for primary, 71 exposed for safety. | Individual participant, not number of walks or adverse events.','Retention and treatment persistence are different measures.'),
('Procedures; Results p5; Figure 1','Events at each contact, safety blood tests monthly, records through 28 days after last visit. | 35 metformin and 36 placebo exposed. | 108 events in 35/35 versus 77 in 33/36; hospital admission 12/35 versus 3/36; diarrhea 19/35 versus 9/36. | Medication stopped for adverse event in 10 versus 3, and out-of-range safety bloods in 2 versus 1; one metformin death judged unrelated. | Four-month treatment plus stated follow-up.','Hospitalization imbalance is important but does not prove each admission was caused by the drug.'),
('Methods; Discussion; Figure 2','Strong masking and retention; small trial, low adherence and short duration. | Randomization reduces baseline confounding; per-protocol estimates can be selected. | GAP: Published protocol identified but separate full protocol/SAP and appendix not acquired in this packet. | No adjustment for multiple secondary/subgroup tests. | Precise enough to exclude targeted primary benefit, not all smaller effects or uncommon harms. | Figure 2 shows baseline-speed interaction P=.030 while prose says no significant subgroup differences; retain discrepancy and do not promote the subgroup.','Null primary result and tolerability matter more than isolated exploratory findings.'),
('Eligibility; Discussion','Very old, predominantly White adults with substantial functional limitation, without diabetes. | 1500 mg/day immediate release without titration. | Walking, strength and function; not generalized healthspan. | Cannot extend directly to younger adults, diabetes care or gradual titration.','Does not justify stopping metformin prescribed for another established indication.'),
('ISRCTN29932357; CP018','MET-PREVENT independent RCT. | CP018 discusses the planned trial; this is its later completed result. | No duplicate participant count via narrative review. | Authors searched PubMed through September 2024; this assessment reconciles existing guide sources.','Do not present CP018\'s trial-underway statement as current.'),
('Byline; disclosures; role of funder','Miles Witham, Claire McDonald, Nina Wilson and colleagues. | Newcastle/Gateshead NHS and university collaborators. | NIHR Newcastle Biomedical Research Centre; philanthropic mechanistic support from Alan Halsall. | ModePharma prepared matched bottles; Aurobindo product identified. | Witham disclosed Rejuvenate Biomed consulting; Clegg disclosed grants/roles; funder and sponsor oversaw design/conduct, not analysis or publication decision.','No blanket no-funder-role or no-conflict statement is appropriate.'),
('Full PDF; references','Published Lancet Healthy Longevity 2025, DOI 10.1016/j.lanhl.2025.100695. | Full ten-page report acquired; primary table and subgroup figure visually inspected. | GAP: Appendix and separate SAP not present; main flow and safety data support the bounded conclusion. | ISRCTN29932357 identified; published protocol DOI 10.1136/bmjopen-2022-061823. | No correction/retraction identified in retrieved current records; subgroup discrepancy retained. | November 3–4, 2026.','This does not claim an independent participant-level audit.'),
('G-METFORMIN physical-function chapter','Sarcopenia, frailty and geroprotection claims. | Contradicts improvement in walk speed for the tested population and regimen. | Authors conclude poor tolerance and no benefit; editorial conclusion is similarly bounded.','Retain hospitalization and discontinuation findings alongside efficacy.'),
('Synthesis','No demonstrated mobility benefit, with a substantial tolerability burden. | Different doses, titration, populations and durations remain separate questions. | Well-powered studies in justified alternative populations with prespecified function and safety outcomes would be needed.','Mechanistic anti-aging rationale cannot override this direct trial.')
], 'High follow-up retention makes the null primary result informative despite frequent treatment discontinuation. The reported primary interval excludes the targeted 0.1 m/s benefit. Tolerability and hospital admissions require prominent discussion, without attributing every event causally.')
